%S A401607 1,8,25,9,4,3,13,2,6,19,5,18,41,72,113,73,42,32,61,33,14,34,62,50,85,
%T A401607 51,26,10,27,52,86,98,145,99,84,128,181,129,112,162,221,163,114,74,43,
%U A401607 20,28,53,87,130,182,200,265,201,146,100,63,35,15
%N A401607 Squares visited by a knight moving on a diagonally numbered Q1 board and always taking the available unvisited square with the shortest distance to the bisecting line of Q1. In the case of a tie, first squares with the shortest Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1) are preferred, second the square with smaller row index is preferred. Starting square is labeled 1.
%C A401607 Board is numbered as follows:
%C A401607    1  2  4  7 11 16 ...
%C A401607    3  5  8 12 17 ...
%C A401607    6  9 13 18 ...
%C A401607   10 14 19 ...
%C A401607   15 20 ...
%C A401607   21 ...
%C A401607   ...
%C A401607 The knight's tour starts at position (1,1), the first square of the tour. The knight moves in the open field spanned by the positive x- and y-axis, i.e., the first quadrant Q1.
%C A401607 In case the knight has a choice between squares, which have equal distance to the bisecting line, first the square which has the shortest Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1) is preferred. If this first tiebreaking rule still leaves an optional square, the square with its row index being smaller than the row index of the alternative square is chosen to break the tie.
%C A401607 The knight becomes trapped after 58 moves at position (5, 1).
%H A401607 M. F. Hasler, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/Knight_tours">Knight tours</a>, OEIS wiki, Nov. 2019.
%H A401607 N. J. A. Sloane and Brady Haran, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RGQe8waGJ4w">The Trapped Knight</a>, Numberphile video (2019).
%H A401607 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A401607/a401607.svg">Illustration of the path</a>.
%H A401607 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A401607/a401607_1.csv.txt">Array after the knight was trapped as csv-file</a>.
%H A401607 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A401607/a401607.py.txt">Python program</a>.
%e A401607 Board after 17 steps: (0's are unvisited squares).
%e A401607   1   8   5   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A401607   6  11   2   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A401607   9   4   7  12   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A401607   0   0  10   3   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A401607   0   0   0   0  13   0   0   0 ...
%e A401607   0   0   0  17   0   0  14   0 ...
%e A401607   0   0   0   0   0  16   0   0 ...
%e A401607   0   0   0   0   0   0   0  15 ...
%e A401607   ...
%e A401607 Positions are (row, column).
%e A401607 a(1) = 1 because 1 is at position (1,1) which is numbered 1.
%e A401607 a(2) = 8 because 2 is at position (2,3) which is numbered 8. In this case, the subordinate tiebreaking rule chooses (2,3) over the other available candidate (3,2), because both positions have equal Euclidean distance to the starting square (1,1) as well as to the bisecting line, but (2,3) exhibits the smaller row index.
%e A401607 a(3) = 25 because 3 is at position (4,4) which is numbered 25.
%e A401607 a(17) = 42 because 17 is at position (6,4) which is numbered 42. In this case, the first tiebreaking rule chooses (6,4) over the other available candidates (5,7) and (6,8) and (9,7) with the same distance to the bisecting line because position (6,4) is closer to the starting square (1,1).
